s)\n- This way, the most constrained decisions are made early, and pruning is more effective\n\nFor the topological sort with heuristics:\n1. Compute topological levels (distance from root)\n2. Within each level, sort by: number of valid rooms (ascending), then duration (ascending)\n\nThis ensures that events with fewer options are scheduled first, which helps the backtracking prune more effectively.\n\nOK, let me finally write the code. I've been thinking for way too long.\n\nOne more thing: the `findEarliestSlot` function needs the room assignments sorted by start time. I'll maintain this invariant.\n\nAlso, the `insertSorted` function needs to insert a placement in the correct position to maintain the sorted order. I'll use binary search for this.\n\nLet me write the code now.\n\n```typescript\n// src/types.ts\nexport interface Event {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n  duration: number;\n  attendees?: number;\n  prerequisites?: string[];\n  preferredRoom?: string;\n}\n\nexport interface Room {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n  capacity: number;\n}\n\nexport interface ScheduleOptions {\n  startTime: number;\n  endTime: number;\n  gap?: number;\n}\n\nexport interface Assignment {\n  eventId: string;\n  roomId: string;\n  startTime: number;\n  endTime: number;\n}\n\nexport interface ScheduleResult {\n  assignments: Assignment[];\n  unscheduled: string[];\n}\n```\n\n```typescript\n// src/scheduler.ts\nimport type { Event, Room, ScheduleOptions, Assignment, ScheduleResult } from './types.js';\n\ninterface Placement {\n  roomId: string;\n  startTime: number;\n  endTime: number;\n}\n\nexport class CyclicDependencyError extends Error {\n  constructor(message: string) {\n    super(message);\n    this.name = 'CyclicDependencyError';\n  }\n}\n\nfunction detectCycle(events: Event[], eventMap: Map<string, Event>): void {\n  const WHITE = 0, GRAY = 1, BLACK = 2;\n  const color = new Map<string, number>();\n  \n  for (const event of events) {\n    color.set(event.id, WHITE);\n  }\n  \n  function dfs(id: string): void {\n    const c = color.get(id);\n    if (c === BLACK) return;\n    if (c === GRAY) {\n      throw new CyclicDependencyError(`Cyclic dependency detected involving event ${id}`);\n    }\n    \n    color.set(id, GRAY);\n    const event = eventMap.get(id);\n    if (event?.prerequisites) {\n      for (const prereqId of event.prerequisites) {\n        // Only check prerequisites that are in our event list\n        if (color.has(prereqId)) {\n          dfs(prereqId);\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    color.set(id, BLACK);\n  }\n  \n  for (const event of events) {\n    if (color.get(event.id) === WHITE) {\n      dfs(event.id);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction topologicalSort(events: Event[], eventMap: Map<string, Event>): Event[] {\n  const visited = new Set<string>();\n  const result: Event[] = [];\n  \n  function visit(event: Event): void {\n    if (visited.has(event.id)) return;\n    visited.add(event.id);\n    \n    if (event.prerequisites) {\n      for (const prereqId of event.prerequisites) {\n        const prereq = eventMap.get(prereqId);\n        if (prereq && !visited.has(prereq.id)) {\n          visit(prereq);\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    \n    result.push(event);\n  }\n  \n  for (const event of events) {\n    visit(event);\n  }\n  \n  return result;\n}\n\nfunction findEarliestSlot(\n  sortedAssignments: Placement[],\n  earliestStart: number,\n  duration: number,\n  gap: number,\n  windowEnd: number\n): number | null {\n  let start = earliestStart;\n  \n  for (const a of sortedAssignments) {\n    if (start + duration > windowEnd) return null;\n    \n    // Can we fit before this assignment?\n    if (start + duration + gap <= a.startTime) {\n      return start;\n    }\n    \n    // Can't fit before, move start past this assignment\n    if (a.endTime + gap > start) {\n      start = a.endTime + gap;\n    }\n  }\n  \n  if (start + duration > windowEnd) return null;\n  return start;\n}\n\nfunction insertSorted(arr: Placement[], placement: Placement): void {\n  let lo = 0, hi = arr.length;\n  while (lo < hi) {\n    const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;\n    if (arr[mid].startTime < placement.startTime) {\n      lo = mid + 1;\n    } else {\n      hi = mid;\n    }\n  }\n  arr.splice(lo, 0, placement);\n}\n\nfunction removeFromSorted(arr: Placement[], placement: Placement): void {\n  let lo = 0, hi = arr.length;\n  while (lo < hi) {\n    const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;\n    if (arr[mid].startTime < placement.startTime) {\n      lo = mid + 1;\n    } else {\n      hi = mid;\n    }\n  }\n  // Check around lo for exact match\n  for (let i = Math.max(0, lo - 1); i <= Math.min(arr.length - 1, lo + 1); i++) {\n    if (arr[i].startTime === placement.startTime && arr[i].endTime === placement.endTime && arr[i].roomId === placement.roomId) {\n      arr.splice(i, 1);\n      return;\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nexport function schedule(events: Event[], rooms: Room[], options: ScheduleOptions): ScheduleResult {\n  const gap = options.gap ?? 15;\n  const eventMap = new Map(events.map(e => [e.id, e]));\n  \n  // Detect cycles\n  detectCycle(events, eventMap);\n  \n  // Topological sort\n  const topoOrder = topologicalSort(events, eventMap);\n  \n  // Precompute valid rooms for each event\n  const validRoomsMap = new Map<string, Room[]>();\n  for (const event of events) {\n    const attendees = event.attendees ?? 1;\n    const valid = rooms.filter(r => r.capacity >= attendees);\n    // Sort: preferred room first, then by capacity ascending (smallest adequate first)\n    valid.sort((a, b) => {\n      if (a.id === event.preferredRoom && b.id !== event.preferredRoom) return -1;\n      if (b.id === event.preferredRoom && a.id !== event.preferredRoom) return 1;\n      return a.capacity - b.capacity;\n    });\n    validRoomsMap.set(event.id, valid);\n  }\n  \n  // Backtracking solver\n  const NODE_LIMIT = 500000;\n  let nodesExplored = 0;\n  \n  const best = { count: 0, assignments: new Map<string, Placement>() };\n  const assignments = new Map<string, Placement>();\n  const roomSchedule = new Map<string, Placement[]>();\n  let scheduledCount = 0;\n  \n  function solve(index: number): void {\n    if (nodesExplored >= NODE_LIMIT) return;\n    nodesExplored++;\n    \n    if (index >= topoOrder.length) {\n      if (scheduledCount > best.count) {\n        best.count = scheduledCount;\n        best.assignments = new Map(assignments);\n      }\n      return;\n    }\n    \n    // Pruning\n    const remaining = topoOrder.length - index;\n    if (scheduledCount + remaining <= best.count) return;\n    \n    const event = topoOrder[index];\n    \n    // Check prerequisites\n    let earliestStart = options.startTime;\n    let prereqMet = true;\n    if (event.prerequisites && event.prerequisites.length > 0) {\n      for (const prereqId of event.prerequisites) {\n        const prereqAssign = assignments.get(prereqId);\n        if (!prereqAssign) {\n          prereqMet = false;\n          break;\n        }\n        earliestStart = Math.max(earliestStart, prereqAssign.endTime + gap);\n      }\n    }\n    \n    let canSchedule = false;\n    \n    // Try to schedule the event\n    if (prereqMet && earliestStart + event.duration <= options.endTime) {\n      const validRooms = validRoomsMap.get(event.id) ?? [];\n      \n      for (const room of validRooms) {\n        const roomAssns = roomSchedule.get(room.id) ?? [];\n        const slot = findEarliestSlot(roomAssns, earliestStart, event.duration, gap, options.endTime);\n        \n        if (slot !== null) {\n          canSchedule = true;\n          const placement: Placement = { roomId: room.id, startTime: slot, endTime: slot + event.duration };\n          \n          // Place\n          assignments.set(event.id, placement);\n          if (!roomSchedule.has(room.id)) {\n            roomSchedule.set(room.id, [placement]);\n          } else {\n            insertSorted(roomSchedule.get(room.id)!, placement);\n          }\n          scheduledCount++;\n          \n          solve(index + 1);\n          \n          // Unplace\n          assignments.delete(event.id);\n          removeFromSorted(roomSchedule.get(room.id)!, placement);\n          if (roomSchedule.get(room.id)?.length === 0) {\n            roomSchedule.delete(room.id);\n          }\n          scheduledCount--;\n          \n          if (best.count === events.length || nodesExplored >= NODE_LIMIT) return;\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    \n    // Try not scheduling this event\n    solve(index + 1);\n  }\n  \n  solve(0);\n  \n  // Build result\n  const result: ScheduleResult = { assignments: [], unscheduled: [] };\n  for (const event of events) {\n    const placement = best.assignments.get(event.id);\n    if (placement) {\n      result.assignments.push({\n        eventId: event.id,\n        roomId: placement.roomId,\n        startTime: placement.startTime,\n        endTime: placement.endTime,\n      });\n    } else {\n      result.unscheduled.push(event.id);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nWait, I just realized there's an issue with my backtracking approach. When I try the \"skip\" option, I always explore it. But this means for every event, I'm branching into (number of valid rooms + 1) options. For N events with R valid rooms each, the total search space is (R+1)^N. With R=2 and N=20, that's 3^20 ≈ 3.5 billion. Way too much.\n\nThe key issue is the \"skip\" option. If I remove it and only try to schedule or skip when no valid placement exists, the search space is R^N (just trying different rooms). With R=2 and N=20, that's about 1 million. With pruning, much less.\n\nBut without the \"skip\" option, I can't handle the case where scheduling an event blocks more events than it adds. Is there a way to handle this without the exponential blowup?\n\nOption 1: Only try \"skip\" for events that have very few valid placements (e.g., only 1 valid room). This limits the branching.\n\nOption 2: First run the greedy (always schedule if possible), then if there are unscheduled events, try removing scheduled events one at a time to see if we can schedule more.\n\nOption 3: Use the greedy result as the initial best, then only explore branches that might beat it.\n\nI think option 3 is the best. Let me implement it:\n\n1. First, run the greedy (schedule every event if possible, never skip)\n2. This gives us a baseline solution\n3. Then, run the backtracking with the greedy result as the initial best\n4. The pruning `scheduledCount + remaining <= best.count` will be very effective because the best is already good\n5. Only branches that might beat the greedy will be explored\n\nThis should make the backtracking much faster because:\n- The greedy gives a good lower bound\n- Most branches will be pruned early\n- The \"skip\" option will only be explored when it might lead to a better solution\n\nLet me implement this.\n\nActually, with the greedy as the initial best, the pruning `scheduledCount + remaining <= best.count` will prune most branches. If the greedy scheduled K out of N events, then any branch with scheduledCount + remaining <= K will be pruned. At the root, remaining = N, so we only explore branches where scheduledCount + N > K, which is always true. But as we go deeper, the pruning becomes more effective.\n\nAt depth d, remaining = N - d. If we've scheduled s events so far, we need s + (N - d) > K to continue. Since the greedy schedules K events, and at depth d we've tried to schedule d events, if we've scheduled s < d events (some were skipped), we need s + N - d > K. If the greedy scheduled most events (K close to N), then s + N - d > K is s > K - N + d = d - (N - K). So we need to have scheduled at least d - (N - K) events out of d to continue. If N - K is small (few unscheduled events), this is very effective.\n\nFor example, if N = 20 and K = 18 (greedy scheduled 18 out of 20), then at depth d, we need s > d - 2. So at depth 10, we need to have scheduled at least 9 events. This means we can skip at most 1 event in the first 10. The pruning is very effective.\n\nBut if K = 10 (only half scheduled), the pruning is less effective. At depth 10, we need s > 10 - 10 = 0. So we can skip up to 10 events. The pruning is not very helpful.\n\nIn this case, the backtracking might be slow. But with the node limit, it will terminate.\n\nOK, let me implement this hybrid approach:\n1. Greedy as initial solution\n2. Backtracking with the greedy as initial best and node limit\n\nLet me also think about the event ordering for the backtracking. The topological sort gives a partial order. Within the topological order, I should process events in an order that makes the backtracking more efficient:\n- Most constrained first (fewer valid rooms, tighter time constraints)\n- This way, the most impactful decisions are made early\n\nBut the topological sort might not naturally order events this way. Let me adjust the topological sort to use heuristics within each level.\n\nActually, for the backtracking, the ordering doesn't matter as much because we're trying all possibilities. But a good ordering makes the pruning more effective because it finds good solutions early.\n\nLet me implement the greedy first, then the backtracking.\n\nActually, let me simplify. Let me implement just the backtracking with node limit, without a separate greedy phase. The backtracking will naturally find the greedy solution first (by always trying to schedule before skipping), and then explore alternatives.\n\nBut wait, the order of room selection matters. If I try the preferred room first, the greedy solution will be found quickly. And with that as the best, the pruning will be effective.\n\nOK, here's my final plan:\n\n1. Topological sort with heuristic ordering (most constrained first within levels)\n2. Backtracking solver:\n   - For each event in order, try all valid rooms (preferred first, then smallest adequate)\n   - For each room, find the earliest slot\n   - Try scheduling the event, then try skipping\n   - Prune branches that can't beat the current best\n   - Node limit for safety\n3. Return the best solution found\n\nThe key optimization is that the backtracking will find the greedy solution first (because it tries scheduling before skipping), and then explore alternatives only when they might beat the greedy.\n\nLet me write the code now.\n\nOne more thing: I need to handle the case where a prerequisite event is not in the events list. If event B has prerequisite A, but A is not in the events list, then B can't be scheduled (A is not scheduled, so the prerequisite is not met).\n\nBut wait, should this be an error? The spec doesn't say. Let me just add B to unscheduled if its prerequisite is not in the events list.\n\nActually, re-reading the spec: \"prerequisites?: string[]; // event IDs that must finish before this starts\". If a prerequisite event ID doesn't correspond to any event in the list, the prerequisite can never be satisfied, so the event can't be scheduled. It should go to unscheduled, not throw an error.\n\nBut for cycle detection, we only need to check events that are in the list. If a prerequisite references a non-existent event, it's not a cycle.\n\nOK, let me handle this in the solver: if a prerequisite is not in the assignments map (either not scheduled or not in the events list), the event can't be scheduled.\n\nLet me also think about what happens if an event has an empty prerequisites array vs undefined. The spec says `prerequisites?: string[]`, so undefined and empty array should be treated the same (no prerequisites).\n\nOK, I think I have a complete understanding. Let me write the code now.\n\nFor the tests, I'll write:\n1. Basic scheduling test\n2. Capacity constraint test\n3. Time window constraint test\n4. Gap constraint test\n5. Prerequisite constraint test\n6. Preferred room test\n7. Cycle detection test\n8. Maximization test (where greedy in input order fails)\n9. Self-reference cycle test\n\nLet me code everything now.\n\nActually, let me also think about edge cases:\n- Empty events list -> empty schedule\n- Empty rooms list -> all events unscheduled\n- Event with duration 0 -> should be scheduled (starts and ends at the same time)\n- Gap of 0 -> events can be back-to-back\n- Event with attendees 0 or undefined -> defaults to 1\n\nWait, what about an event with duration 0? It starts and ends at the same time. Does it need a gap? The gap is \"end of one event to start of the next.\" If an event has duration 0, its start and end are the same. Two 0-duration events at the same time in the same room would have 0 gap between them. If gap > 0, they can't be at the same time.\n\nActually, with 0 duration, the event fits anywhere. findEarliestSlot would return earliestStart. Two 0-duration events at the same time would have: first.endTime + gap = start + gap > start (the second's start). So they'd need gap minutes between them. That seems right.\n\nWhat about an event with duration longer than the time window? It can't be scheduled. findEarliestSlot would return null.\n\nOK, I think I've covered all the edge cases. Let me write the code.\n\nWait, one more thing about the gap for prerequisites. The spec says:\n\n\"The gap between prerequisite events also applies (A's end time + gap <= B's start time).\"\n\nThis means the gap applies between prerequisite events, regardless of whether they're in the same room. So if A is in room 1 and B is in room 2, we still need A.endTime + gap <= B.startTime.\n\nI already handle this by computing earliestStart based on prerequisites. Good.\n\nBut what about the gap for events in different rooms that are NOT prerequisites? The gap only applies to events in the same room. So if C is in room 1 and D is in room 2, no gap is needed between them (unless they have a prerequisite relationship).\n\nOK, I think I have everything figured out. Let me write the code.\n\nHmm, actually, I want to reconsider the algorithm one more time. The backtracking with \"skip\" option has 2^N branching. Even with pruning and node limits, it might not find optimal solutions for larger inputs.\n\nLet me consider an alternative: instead of backtracking with skip, use a different formulation.\n\n**Approach: Greedy with multiple orderings**\n\n1. Generate multiple valid orderings of events (all respecting topological order)\n2. For each ordering, run the greedy scheduler (always schedule, never skip)\n3. Return the best result\n\nThe key is generating good orderings. I can use:\n- Topological order with different within-level orderings:\n  - Shortest first\n  - Longest first\n  - Most constrained first\n  - Random shuffling\n- For each ordering, try different room preferences:\n  - Preferred room first\n  - Smallest adequate room first\n  - Largest adequate room first\n\nThis gives us K orderings, each taking O(N * R * log(N)) time. Total: O(K * N * R * log(N)).\n\nFor K = 10-20 orderings, this is very fast and will likely find good solutions.\n\nThe advantage is that by varying the ordering, we implicitly explore the \"skip\" option: if event A is tried later in one ordering and earlier in another, the later events in the first ordering have more room.\n\nBut this doesn't explicitly try skipping. If an event can be scheduled but shouldn't be (because it blocks others), this approach won't find that.\n\nHmm, but I can add a \"skip\" ordering: for each event, try an ordering where that event is placed last. This way, other events get priority.\n\nActually, let me combine both approaches:\n1. Run multiple greedy orderings to find a good initial solution\n2. If not all events are scheduled, try backtracking with the initial solution as the lower bound\n\nThis way, the backtracking starts with a good lower bound and only explores branches that might improve it.\n\nOK, I think I'm overcomplicating this. Let me just implement the backtracking with node limit and move on. If it's too slow for some test cases, I'll optimize.\n\nLet me also consider: for the \"maximizing\" requirement, what if