e most correct approach and with the node limit, it won't take too long.\n\nHere's my final implementation plan:\n\n```\n1. detectCycle() - throws on cycle\n2. topologicalSort() - returns events in topological order\n3. findEarliestSlot() - finds earliest available slot in a room\n4. schedule() - main function:\n   a. Detect cycles\n   b. Topological sort\n   c. Precompute valid rooms\n   d. Run backtracking solver with node limit\n   e. Return best result\n```\n\nLet me write the code now. For real this time. No more thinking.\n\nWait, one last thing. I need to make sure the backtracking tries the \"best\" options first so that it finds good solutions quickly and prunes effectively.\n\nFor room selection order:\n1. Preferred room (if valid)\n2. Smallest adequate room (to save larger rooms)\n3. Other rooms by capacity ascending\n\nFor event ordering:\n1. Topological order (prerequisites first)\n2. Within the same level, most constrained first (fewer valid rooms)\n3. Within the same constraint level, shorter duration first (fits more)\n\nLet me implement this ordering in the topological sort.\n\nActually, for the backtracking, I should also consider: what if I process events in a different topological order? Different topological orderings can lead to different solutions.\n\nBut exploring all topological orderings is too expensive. Let me just use one good ordering and rely on the backtracking to find alternatives.\n\nOK, let me write the code now.\n\nActually, I realize I should think about one more thing. The spec says:\n\n\"Preferred room: When an event specifies a preferredRoom, honor it if possible without violating other constraints.\"\n\nThis means: if the preferred room is valid (capacity OK) and has an available slot, use it. Only use a different room if the preferred room doesn't have a slot or isn't valid.\n\nSo the preferred room should be tried FIRST. If it works, use it. Only if it doesn't work, try other rooms.\n\nBut for the backtracking, I should sti